- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a trick for monotone optima?
- How would you adapt if windows could overlap or if k is variable?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

